in beijing's great hall of the people  almost 3 000 communist party officials  closed china's national people's  congress  by voting on a roadmap for the next five  to 15 years  the priorities achieving six percent gdp  growth  increasing the domestic market and  creating 11 million new jobs  we would certainly be happy to see  robust growth  but we are also aware of the uncertainty  involving chinese economic rebounds  global economic development is also  uncertain  also approved by nearly all the  delegates with just one abstention  a bill to overhaul hong kong's political  system  the reforms will see seats added to the  territory's legislative council  allocated mostly to pro-beijing figures  a committee will be established to vet  those who hold public office  ensuring they're loyal to the chinese  government or considered patriots by  beijing  ted huy a former opposition politician  who fled hong kong  fearing punishment under beijing's new  national security law  has just arrived in australia and says  he's heartbroken by the reforms  it took like decades to  uh confirm and to develop uh  to finish that the design of our  constitutions  but it only took uh two or three months  to have to have that totally destroyed  and so we  we feel betrayed of course traded  and we feel miserable  of losing our freedoms the u.s is  leading growing international criticism  about the changes  saying they're an attack on hong kong's  democratic freedoms  strained ties between the two countries  were a focal point of this year's  national people's congress  washington has called beijing a  strategic threat china's leaders have  responded by pushing for a more  innovation-driven economy  china now has much more focus  on using domestic resources and  increasing domestic  investment in basic research  like biology semiconductors  so i think now the competition between  china and the u.s  will stay for a long time it makes  economic sense for china to be more  self-reliant  president xi jinping has called for an  increase to china's military capability  china's leaders say they want to work  with the biden administration to expand  common interests top officials from the  u.s and china are expected to meet in  alaska next week  to discuss a range of issues but a long  list of disagreements threatens to  overshadow the talks  including beijing's treatment of muslim  uighurs in xinjiang  china's communist party says its  approach to the uyghurs and hong kong  are strictly domestic affairs  and it won't be swayed by so-called  foreign interference  katrina you al jazeera beijing
 

